    "Public genre reference for the tamborzão loop — the atabaque/timbal percussion pattern funk carioca has been built on since the late 1990s. Parameters are extrapolated from the documented rhythm, not measured from any recording."
  ],
  "notes": "The tamborzão: a percussion loop, not a kick-and-snare grid. Six hand-drum lanes — conga, timbale, woodblock, shaker, rim and a generic perc — run at the densest rate anywhere in this dataset and land anywhere on the 16th grid, so the subdivision is carried by drums rather than by hats, which are close to absent here. Under them the bass drum refuses the quarters: it anchors the 1 and then goes to the a of 2 and the and of 3, and everything else it plays leans offbeat. The backbeat is an answer rather than the engine, ghosted heavily on the e and a slots. Harmony is almost nothing — a one- or two-chord minor vamp so the chant and the drums have the record to themselves — and the bass is deep, simple and locked to the kick. ⚠ confidence is medium: the compendium has no funk carioca section, so the tempo, register and harmony numbers are extrapolated from ch.2 §10's club-influenced grammar and from the publicly documented shape of the tamborzão.",
